Carmen Cruickshank: Did you hear that they're signing off?

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:19.000
Fallon Looney: I think so. I'm not too familiar with them, just because I don't think we were planning on transferring them over, or if we were, we, like, didn't, um, get started on it. I don't think we were planning on it, but yeah, is there any…

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Yeah.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Mm-hmm.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Fallon Looney: insight with them, like.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:34.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Yeah, I'm just glad, like, so… there was an email that Vanessa sent just to Whitney, and it was just like, oh, uh, thank you for your services, but we're not gonna continue forward with you guys, which honestly…

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.000
Fallon Looney: Mmhm.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Carmen Cruickshank: They basically for the past like few months, not few months, I'd say like majority of the year.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:53.000
Carmen Cruickshank: we're just rude over email, like, she was telling us what to do in Google, yeah, like, as if, like, she's an expert, where it's like, okay, like, if you don't want our services, like, don't get our services. Uh…

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.000
Fallon Looney: Oh, yes.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Carmen Cruickshank: And so finally, like, she was like, oh, okay, yeah, we're just going to continue for another direction. So I'm definitely happy about.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Oh.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:15.000
Carmen Cruickshank: I'm just curious how their accounts are. They spend, like, 5K a month on Google Ads, which honestly, like, they should do more meta, because also they do not have a strong landing page, so it's like those things are out of our control. Uh, but I'm definitely glad that they're not.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:08.000
Fallon Looney: Yeah.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Here forever, so…

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:25.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Yeah, that's what confuses me, though. I don't know if you noticed that in the other agency you were in, but, like, people hire you guys…

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:30.000
Carmen Cruickshank: And then it's like, no, you're doing this wrong, da-da-da-da-da.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.000
Carmen Cruickshank: It just makes me want to fire them, even though I can't, you know.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:51.000
Fallon Looney: I know. I've definitely… I've had some crazy experiences, and I will say, I mean, granted, I haven't really worked with, like, actually being super involved in the clients yet. I've just done, you know, like, a couple of calls and started taking over.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Mm-hmm.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.000
Fallon Looney: But at other…

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:11.000
Fallon Looney: agencies that I've worked for. The retainer has been super high. That like they're paying the like. I've worked with agencies where the retainer that they're charging clients is like 5,000 to $5,500 a month, like just.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Mm.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:27.000
Fallon Looney: for the service, and then, on top of it, they have to spend at least 5 K. A month on Meta. And so they were having, like, you know, much bigger budgets, and some of them were in different industries. So like.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Okay.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:20.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Hmm.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:39.000
Fallon Looney: coaches, course creators, like, business coaches, um, you know, digital products and things like that. And so, they were spending at a much…

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.000
Fallon Looney: higher level.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:57.000
Fallon Looney: And they would get very, like, the main point of contact would be the CEO, which here is, like, still pretty similar. Like, we're usually talk… we're not talking to, like, a marketing person, like you would in corporate. Um, but, like, your point of contact would directly be the CEO.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Hmm.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:14.000
Fallon Looney: the creative that we were putting out was like they're like they're the brand like, if you're a business coach, it's like you are the brand as the founder, and they would be. There was a lot more likelihood of them being like insanely particular with the creative, because.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:28.000
Fallon Looney: part of it is also, like, their likeness and their brand, and then they're spending a lot more, so then they became a lot more… there were instances, not all, it was very rare, but, like, I've definitely encountered clients that.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Mmhm.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.000
Fallon Looney: You know.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:47.000
Fallon Looney: got like that for sure, and when they are spending more to your point like they're spending 5 K. A month. It just adds up like super fast. And then I think that becomes triggering for them if they don't feel in control, or.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:53.000
Fallon Looney: if something else is at play, but yeah, I've definitely seen it, for sure.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:51.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Mm-hmm.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:09:02.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Yeah, definitely. That's good, though, like, having high retainer, though. Because I guess maybe they're hopeful that it would work, so they want to stay more, or do you think it was, like, really good customer service you guys would give?

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.000
Fallon Looney: Yes.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:03.000
Fallon Looney: Um…

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:09.000
Fallon Looney: I think it was a mix… it's a mix of both. I mean, I think that there was, like.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:21.000
Fallon Looney: Obviously, quality of service was there. I think the reason, another reason for the big retainer was that, like, the structure of it was everybody on the team is, like.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:37.000
Fallon Looney: full time, working 8 HA day, taking on more clients, higher spend. And like, especially on Meta, the more that you spend, the more creative is required. So there's just like more output that needs to happen. So like more volume of creatives like running.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:53.000
Fallon Looney: you know, 10 to 20 ad creatives at a time for one campaign for one client. And so it's like, it's also like the output of the work, and just like the margin of the business that is is a part of it, like the cost of like.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:55.000
Fallon Looney: For the agency owner.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:56.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Hmm.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:10.000
Fallon Looney: and also like they would, you know, which I think we do to an extent here as well, but like writing custom copy like I would write like from scratch landing page, copy email copy.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:25.000
Fallon Looney: things like that. So it just very like it would touch every point in the process and like creating the funnels for them. So that's also like part of the retainer with those types of agencies is like, you're just doing more of.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:36.000
Fallon Looney: like, doing more work for them, so it's more, like, full service. Um, I think we're pretty full service here as well, um, but I think that, like.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:30.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Mm-hmm.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.000
Fallon Looney: With.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:40.000
Fallon Looney: Other places I've been to, it's like…

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:55.000
Fallon Looney: doing everything from scratch. And there'd be weekly reports sent every Friday versus the monthly reports calls like every week or every other week, instead of once monthly, just because when they're spending more money, it's like.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:07.000
Fallon Looney: there's… you're getting data faster when you're spending more money, so there's, like, more volume to, like, talk to them about and draw conclusions faster. So a little bit of it's, like, kind of relative to the industries, too, I think.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:04.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Hmm.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:08.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Okay.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:09.000
Fallon Looney: Yeah.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:16.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Yeah, that makes sense. Yeah, I'm like, it makes me feel almost like spoiled here because it's not as much like that as demanding. But wow.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:14.000
Fallon Looney: Mmhm.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.000
Fallon Looney: Mm-hmm.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.000
Carmen Cruickshank: That's a lot. Was that like…

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.000
Fallon Looney: For sure, yeah.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:27.000
Carmen Cruickshank: And you don't have to answer this if you don't want to, but was it, like, more of the people that worked there, like, more burnt out? Or was it just something that, like, people just kind of got.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:45.000
Fallon Looney: Um, I mean, I definitely got, like, burnt out, for sure. Um, especially, like, in my… in my role, I, like, worked my way up, where I was working directly at two different agencies, working directly, like, under the CEOs of the agency, so then you have the.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Mmhm.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:12:00.000
Fallon Looney: you know the which like was super rewarding. But you carry like with leading the team. You're carrying like the the client. And then you're also carrying the team and like supporting what the team needs and what the client needs, which.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:16.000
Fallon Looney: is a part of leadership. But I definitely think that the team would feel overwhelmed, because then you have all of the responsibility, because, like, if the landing page isn't working well, you wrote the landing page, and I'm paying you $5,000 a month. And now the landing page isn't working. So there was always like.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:27.000
Fallon Looney: that room, and it's very client-dependent. There's a lot of entrepreneurs out there that were like, yeah, it's part of the process, it's learning, it's, like, growing and testing.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:43.000
Fallon Looney: But then there are other clients that might be in more of like a scarcity mindset that then put more pressure onto the team. And yeah, it can be like a fast track to burn out. But I will say that also comes to from like.

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:54.000
Fallon Looney: the leadership team. Like, I think that, you know, the culture that is here, it, like, is so much better than other.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:13:02.000
Fallon Looney: places that I've been for sure, because, like it all trickles down from the top, for sure.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:00.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Mm-hmm.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:18.000
Carmen Cruickshank: Yeah, wow. It's just interesting hearing from other places. That's just, that's cool though. I feel like you're like so like well equipped and you like know all these things and you're coming here and you're like, you know, but still very, I don't know, focused. Cause when I hear the breakdowns you have, I'm like, that's good. You know what I mean?

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:08.000
Fallon Looney: I know. Yeah.

